From: Alessio Ferri <alessio.ferri@mythread.it>
Date: Thu, 6 Aug 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] b43: fill the per-rate transmit power offsets on N-PHY

b43_nphy_tx_power_ctl_setup() spreads nphy->tx_power_offset[] over the 84
entries of the adjusted power table, four columns of twenty groups: SISO,
CDD, STBC and SDM. That array has no writer. It is declared in phy_n.h,
read in two places and filled nowhere, so those 84 cells go out as zeros
whatever the SPROM says - and they are what the hardware backs each rate
off by, so every rate transmits at the same power.

Fill it. The reference computes the same quantity as the maximum minus the
per-rate target (brcmsmac phy_cmn.c), and the layout lines up cell for
cell: the PPR this driver already builds in b43_nphy_op_recalc_txpower()
holds CCK, OFDM, OFDM CDD and the four MCS groups in exactly the order the
rate indices want them, so it is one pass over __all_rates.

Measured against MMIO captures of wl 6.30.102.7 and wl 7.14 on two boards,
with the per-rate power offsets loaded into the harness from their NVRAM: on
its own this fills the table with values computed from the SPROM alone, which
is not what the vendor driver programs - 4 of the 84 entries agree on one
board and none would on the other. What decides those entries is a pair of
ceilings, one per number of transmit chains, and that is a separate patch
because the ceilings are measured on one radio while this is not: an array
with no writer is wrong everywhere.

Not tested on hardware, like everything else in this series. What it
changes on a device is real though, and worth saying: rates that were all
being given the same power now get the per-rate backoff the SPROM asks
for.

Verified: records #5986-#6157, from
"TBL.WR   id=0x001a off=0x0040 len=84"
Link: https://git.kernel.org/pub/scm/linux/kernel/git/torvalds/linux.git/tree/drivers/net/wireless/broadcom/brcm80211/brcmsmac/phy/phy_cmn.c?id=848acc8ffe1b#n1399
Link: https://git.kernel.org/pub/scm/linux/kernel/git/torvalds/linux.git/tree/drivers/net/wireless/broadcom/brcm80211/brcmsmac/phy/phy_n.c?id=848acc8ffe1b#n17468
Link: https://github.com/aleferri/b43-6362-wip/blob/c365f7b/router-data/dsl-3580l/opinit-ch1-ch6-bw20.decoded
Link: https://github.com/aleferri/b43-6362-wip/blob/c365f7b/router-data/dsl-3580l/nvram.txt

Signed-off-by: Alessio Ferri <alessio.ferri@mythread.it>
---
--- a/drivers/net/wireless/broadcom/b43/phy_n.c
+++ b/drivers/net/wireless/broadcom/b43/phy_n.c
@@ -6477,6 +6477,7 @@ static enum b43_txpwr_result b43_nphy_op_recalc_txpower(struct b43_wldev *dev,
 	struct ieee80211_channel *channel = dev->wl->hw->conf.chandef.chan;
 	struct b43_ppr *ppr = &nphy->tx_pwr_max_ppr;
 	u8 max; /* qdBm */
+	int i;
 
 	if (nphy->tx_pwr_last_recalc_freq == channel->center_freq &&
 	    nphy->tx_pwr_last_recalc_limit == phy->desired_txpower)
@@ -6511,6 +6512,22 @@ static enum b43_txpwr_result b43_nphy_op_recalc_txpower(struct b43_wldev *dev,
 	/* Make sure we didn't go too low */
 	b43_ppr_apply_min(dev, ppr, INT_TO_Q52(8));
 
+	/* The per rate backoff from the maximum, which is what
+	 * b43_nphy_tx_power_ctl_setup() spreads over the 84 entries of the
+	 * adjusted power table. Without this nphy->tx_power_offset[] has no
+	 * writer at all - it is declared, read twice and never filled - so those
+	 * 84 cells go out as zeros however the SPROM is programmed.
+	 *
+	 * The reference computes the same thing as
+	 * "pi->tx_power_max - pi->tx_power_target[rate]"
+	 * (brcmsmac phy_cmn.c), and the layout lines up cell for cell: the PPR
+	 * holds CCK, OFDM, OFDM CDD and the four MCS groups in exactly the order
+	 * the rate indices have them, so it is one pass over __all_rates.
+	 */
+	max = b43_ppr_get_max(dev, ppr);
+	for (i = 0; i < B43_PPR_RATES_NUM; i++)
+		nphy->tx_power_offset[i] = max - ppr->__all_rates[i];
+
 	/* Apply */
 	b43_mac_suspend(dev);
 	b43_nphy_tx_power_ctl_setup(dev);
@@ -7164,6 +7181,16 @@ static int b43_phy_initn(struct b43_wldev *dev)
 		b43_nphy_rssi_cal(dev);
 	}
 
+	/* The transmit power control coefficients go in before the calibrations,
+	 * not after. In the reference this call is the last thing the PHY init
+	 * does and what precedes it only schedules a calibration for later, so
+	 * nothing is emitted between the two; here the calibrations run inline
+	 * at that point, so leaving the call after them put it on the wrong side
+	 * of six thousand operations. The capture has the pair either side: once
+	 * at the end of the init and once at the end of the calibration run.
+	 */
+	b43_nphy_tx_pwr_ctrl_coef_setup(dev);
+
 	if (!((nphy->measure_hold & 0x6) != 0)) {
 		if (b43_current_band(dev->wl) == NL80211_BAND_2GHZ)
 			do_cal = !nphy->iqcal_chanspec_2G.center_freq;
@@ -7226,7 +7253,6 @@ static int b43_phy_initn(struct b43_wldev *dev)
 		}
 	}
 
-	b43_nphy_tx_pwr_ctrl_coef_setup(dev);
 	b43_nphy_tx_power_ctrl(dev, tx_pwr_state);
 	b43_phy_write(dev, B43_NPHY_TXMACIF_HOLDOFF, 0x0015);
 	b43_phy_write(dev, B43_NPHY_TXMACDELAY, 0x0320);
